\f2\fs25\fsmilli12857 \cf4 \strokec4 What this tool actually is (reasoned, not parroted)\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\f0\fs28 \cf2 \strokec2 It's a\'a0
\f3 scalpel, not an oracle.
\f0 \'a0It doesn't pick winners and it isn't a standalone algo \'97 if two indicators could autonomously print money across all equities, the edge would've been arbitraged to zero. Its job is\'a0
\f1\i execution
\f0\i0 : once a human (later Mastermind) has decided\'a0
\f1\i this vehicle, this regime
\f0\i0 , the tool times the entries and exits to (a) harvest the move, (b) step aside during rotations instead of bleeding through a drawdown while capital flows to the next sector, and (c) kill the opportunity cost of dead money in a stalled name. Your BABA point is the whole argument \'97 B&H is only "unbeatable" with a time machine; in the real distribution of stocks, most are BABAs, not NVDAs, and the alpha is in\'a0
\f1\i not being trapped
\f0\i0 .\
So "alpha" here \uc0\u8800  out-returning a single lucky name. It's\'a0
\f3 opportunity-cost + drawdown avoided across a sequence of rotations
\f0 \'a0\'97 a benchmark like "rotating among fresh setups vs sitting in any one thing," never single-name B&H. The endgame all-weather algo is a\'a0
\f1\i Mastermind-brain
\f0\i0 \'a0problem (many signals + much more confluence); this tool is the high-precision\'a0
\f1\i input
\f0\i0 \'a0to that, refined to be the next-best-thing.\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

