\f2\fs25\fsmilli12857 \cf4 \strokec4 The real work: patch the holes, mechanism-first\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\f0\fs28 \cf2 \strokec2 Your two known holes, and here's the deeper read on the nastier one (JNJ):\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\f3 \cf2 The steep-grind failure is a regime mismatch, not a tuning issue.
\f0 \'a0Your exit logic is\'a0
\f1\i mean-reversion-flavored
\f0\i0 \'a0\'97 it sells oscillator tops. That's correct for an MCD-style 50\'9660\'b0 run with deep, high-amplitude corrections (the oscillator has room to reset, so sell-the-top / rebuy-the-dip works). But a JNJ-style ~70\'b0 low-amplitude grind needs\'a0
\f1\i trend-following
\f0\i0 \'a0behavior \'97 hold until the\'a0
\f3 structure
\f0 \'a0breaks, ignore oscillator tops entirely \'97 because the corrections never go deep enough to re-arm a confluence rebuy. Note the consequence:\'a0
\f3 fix the exit and the rebuy problem largely dissolves
\f0 \'a0\'97 you never needed to rebuy if you never wrongly sold. My crude "hold-through-bull" toggle was a binary stab at this; the real fix is an\'a0
\f3 exit mode that switches on the trend's slope + correction amplitude.
\f0 \
And your per-ticker idea is right\'a0
\f1\i if done as characterization, not curve-fitting
\f0\i0 : profile each name's structural behavior (correction-depth percentiles, slope regimes, who-manipulates-it habits) and adapt thresholds to that \'97 grouped by\'a0
\f3 archetype
\f0 \'a0first (low-vol grinders / high-beta momo / mean-reverters) and validated on held-out time windows so we're adapting to a stock's\'a0
\f1\i nature
\f0\i0 , not fitting noise. That's defensible; per-name return-optimization would not be.\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

